WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.250
Every intrusion has a moment where the attacker stops being a payload on disk and becomes an operator with hands on the keyboard.

2
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:13.370
That happens over a command-and-control channel — and spotting it is how you catch the intrusion mid-flight.

3
00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:22.080
Three words first. A beacon, or implant, is the malware component that keeps the C2 link alive — checking in for orders.

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.980
Jitter is the random delay it adds between check-ins so it doesn't look too regular.

5
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.130
And exfil is simply stolen data leaving the network.

6
00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:39.000
So what gives a C2 channel away? Regular beaconing — connections at a steady interval to the same host.

7
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.780
Jittered timing — almost regular, but with a deliberate random wobble.

8
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:50.790
An odd destination — a newly registered domain, a rare ASN, or a bad reputation.

9
00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:55.280
And a long-lived connection that simply refuses to close.

10
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.600
Exfiltration is the payoff — the data going out the door.

11
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:09.010
Watch for a large outbound transfer, data tunneled over an odd protocol like DNS, or an upload to a destination nobody in your org should be sending to.

12
00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.560
So find the heartbeat, then follow the data.

13
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:22.660
Regular-but-jittered connections to an odd host are the C2; a big outbound to a strange place is the exfil. Catch either and you're mid-intrusion.
